Adaptive bit-pool for SBC (Y2Linux), the counterpart of AAC ABR (patch 0002).

SBC kept its configured bit-pool on a weak link (53 at 44.1 kHz joint stereo,
~328 kbit/s): a sink with a poor radio, such as a cheap SBC-only soundbar, ran
dry and stuttered while other sinks coped with the same rate. With --sbc-abr
the encoder steps the bit-pool down (100%, 85%, 66% of the configured value:
53 -> 45 -> 35, never below the peer's negotiated minimum) when two packets are
queued or a write had to wait, and back up after ~10 s with an empty queue.
libsbc applies a new bit-pool from the next frame on; frames per packet stay
as sized for the configured bit-pool. EncoderStats (patch 0001) reports the
live Bitpool, BitrateKbps, AbrEnabled and AbrAdjustments.
Decisions: src/y2-sbc-abr.h.

